findings.body:
**Keep every request binding for a reused item.**

`entriesFor` keeps only the first request ID per item (`reviewHistory.ts:235`). When two requests bind to one item, a predecessor query for the second request does not mark the item explicit (`:244`). The item can still match by task or branch (`:250-256`), so it is linked under the first request ID (`:261`). The fallback does not add it again (`:460`). The client observation at `:504` then skips the row, because its `requestId` differs from `input.predecessor`. When server ancestry is unknown, for example in a shallow checkout, the supplied ancestor observation is not applied. The projection can then report `possible` with `repeat=false`.

Two bindings to one item are reachable. `reviewAdmission.ts` binds a new request to an existing item on the reused and joined paths (`:288-295`) and on the observed path (`:341-352`), and it appends the request to `observers`.

Store all request IDs per item, and prefer the explicit predecessor alias when it is bound:

<details>
<summary>Suggested fix</summary>

```diff
-  const byItem = new Map<string, string>();
+  const byItem = new Map<string, string[]>();
   for (const [requestId, bindingValue] of requests.entries) {
     const binding = asObject(bindingValue);
     const item = binding === null ? null : binding.get('item');
-    if (typeof item === 'string' && !byItem.has(item)) byItem.set(item, requestId);
+    if (typeof item === 'string') byItem.set(item, [...(byItem.get(item) ?? []), requestId]);
   }
@@
-    const explicit = query.predecessor !== null && byItem.get(itemId(item)) === query.predecessor;
+    const bound = byItem.get(itemId(item)) ?? [];
+    const explicit = query.predecessor !== null && bound.includes(query.predecessor);
@@
-      requestId: byItem.get(itemId(item)) ?? null,
+      requestId: explicit ? query.predecessor : (bound[0] ?? null),
```

</details>
